MQA and GQA as Non-Contextual TPA. Multi-Query Attention (MQA) (Shazeer, 2019) and Grouped Query Attention (GQA) (Ainslie et al., 2023) also emerge naturally from TPA by restricting the head-dimension factors to be non-contextual and low-rank:
• MQA as Rank-1 TPA. In MQA, all heads share a single set of keys/values, corresponding to RK = RV = 1 along the head dimension. Concretely,
Kt = (1, . . . , 1)⊤ ⊗ bK (xt), Vt = (1, . . . , 1)⊤ ⊗ bV (xt),
forces every head to use the same Kt, Vt. Each head retains a distinct query projection, matching the MQA design.
• GQA as Grouped Rank-1 TPA. GQA partitions h heads into G groups, each sharing keys/values within that group. In TPA form, each group g has a dedicated non-contextual factor pair agK , agV ∈
Rh, which acts as a “mask” for the heads in that group. Varying G from 1 to h interpolates from MQA to standard MHA.
Hence, by constraining TPA’s head-dimension factors to be constant masks (one for MQA; multiple for GQA), these popular variants are recovered as special cases.
